      "Augustine was born on 13 November 354 in Thagaste, a town in Roman North Africa, in what is now Algeria. His father, Patricius, was a local official who followed traditional Roman religion; his mother, Monica, was a Christian whose persistent prayer became an important influence on his life. Augustine received a strong education in grammar and rhetoric, first locally and later at Madaura and Carthage, and showed exceptional intellectual ability.",
      "As a young man, Augustine pursued professional success and searched for an explanation of evil, truth and human happiness. He lived for many years with a woman whose name is not recorded, and they had a son, Adeodatus. For roughly nine years he followed Manichaeism, a religious movement that promised an explanation of the conflict between good and evil. He later judged its teaching inadequate, but his search continued; his mother Monica never stopped praying for his conversion.",
      "In 383 Augustine travelled to Rome and then, in 384, obtained a post as professor of rhetoric in Milan. There he heard the preaching of Bishop Ambrose and began to read Scripture with new understanding. Christian teaching, philosophical reading and an increasingly painful awareness of his own divided will gradually changed him. In 386 he decided to become Christian; at Easter in 387 Ambrose baptized Augustine and his son Adeodatus. Monica died at Ostia in 388 while they were preparing to return to Africa.",
      "After returning to North Africa, Augustine sought a life of prayer and study with companions. During a visit to Hippo Regius in 391, the congregation asked him to become a priest; he was ordained and later became bishop there, probably around 395 or 396. His ministry included preaching, pastoral care, writing, resolving disputes and responding to controversies involving Manichaeans, Donatists and Pelagian teachings. He remained an active pastor as well as a major thinker.",
      "Augustine’s many writings include Confessions, a searching account of his spiritual journey, and The City of God, written amid the upheavals following the sack of Rome in 410. He reflected deeply on grace, memory, desire, freedom, Scripture and the human longing for God. As the Vandals besieged Hippo, Augustine died on 28 August 430. The Church honours him as a Doctor of the Church and celebrates his feast on 28 August; his life is remembered as a journey from restless searching to faith and service.",
