      "Francesco Forgione was born on 25 May 1887 in Pietrelcina, a small town in southern Italy, to a farming family. From childhood he was drawn to prayer and spoke of religious experiences that his family and spiritual advisers interpreted cautiously. At the age of fifteen he entered the Capuchin Franciscan Order and took the name Pio. He made his religious profession in 1904 and was ordained a priest in 1910, despite recurring illness that interrupted his formation and required periods of rest at home.",
      "In 1916 Pio was assigned to the Capuchin friary at San Giovanni Rotondo, where he would remain for most of his life. He became known for long hours of prayer, the celebration of Mass, preaching and hearing confessions. Many people travelled to see him, seeking spiritual guidance and reconciliation. His ministry was demanding: he often spent many hours in the confessional and encouraged people to return to the sacraments, prayer and concrete works of charity.",
      "On 20 September 1918, while praying after Mass, Pio reported receiving visible wounds corresponding to the wounds of Christ's Passion, known as the stigmata. The wounds attracted intense attention, curiosity and medical examination. Church authorities investigated reports about him and at different times imposed restrictions on his public ministry while seeking to assess the claims and protect the faithful from sensationalism. Accounts of healings and extraordinary spiritual gifts circulated, but individual supernatural claims should be distinguished from the established facts of his life and ministry.",
      "Pio encouraged the formation of prayer groups and supported the building of the Casa Sollievo della Sofferenza, a hospital opened in San Giovanni Rotondo on 5 May 1956. He saw care for the sick as an essential expression of Christian love and wanted the hospital to combine medical competence with humane attention to patients. His life also included physical suffering, spiritual trials and controversy, and he repeatedly urged people not to treat religious devotion as a substitute for medical care or personal responsibility.",
      "Padre Pio died on 23 September 1968 in San Giovanni Rotondo. Pope John Paul II beatified him on 2 May 1999 and canonised him on 16 June 2002. His feast is celebrated on 23 September. He is remembered for his devotion to the Eucharist, commitment to confession, prayer groups and care for the sick. The Church's recognition of his sanctity rests on his Christian life and ministry; stories about extraordinary events should be read with attention to the distinction between documented history, testimony and devotional interpretation.",
